# VITURE Pro XR glasses -- USB access for Refract's head-tracking SDK and
# hardware controls (brightness, volume, film, the SBS dimension switch).
#
# Without this, the device node is left root:root by the kernel default and
# every one of those fails -- the vendor SDK's init() returns a plain
# false, which surfaces as "SDK init() failed -- are the glasses plugged
# in?" even though lsusb sees the device fine (that only needs read access;
# claiming the interface needs write too).
#
# Refract used to get this for free from XRLinuxDriver's own udev rule, but
# that only holds if XRLinuxDriver (or Breezy Desktop) has been installed at
# some point and never fully removed -- Refract's own conflict handling can
# offer to uninstall it, which takes this rule with it. Refract now ships
# its own so hardware access does not depend on another project's installer
# having run first.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" covers the systemd-logind desktop case (the seat's active
# user gets access automatically, no group membership needed); GROUP is a
# fallback for setups without logind seat management.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="35ca", MODE="0660", GROUP="plugdev", TAG+="uaccess"
